A||||12||Ralfy9862||spark plugs and wires||||1||
Z||000000||Ralfy9862||10-24-2000||10:25 PM||||what is the easiest way to change the sparkplugs and wires on the fiero and i also have a oil leak comming from my distributer whats a way to fix it.||||reg||1
Z||000001||My7Fieros||10-24-2000||10:40 PM||||Spark plugs..I remove the decklid..its EXTREMELY easy to do and helps tremendously. Some people say they dont remove the decklid, try it either/both ways and decide which way is better for you. I then use swivel sockets and remove/replace the plugs. <BR>Wires, do them one at a time.<BR>The oil leak around the distributor is the distributor O-ring. Pull out the dist. and replace the ring...easy easy fix. Might as well replace the pick up coil, ignition controle module, cap and rotor while you're at it...<BR>All are easy to do.<p>[This message has been edited by My7Fieros (edited 10-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||10-24-2000||11:42 PM||||Just be sure to scribe or paint with white out a mark on the deck lid to insure realignment. It is really easy and makes changing plugs 10x simpler. Just remove the 2 bolts on each side of the deck lid and have someone help you take if off and set it down, kinda difficult to do your self.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||10-24-2000||11:49 PM||||Here we go again...<BR>Personally, I wouldn't flip my lid, just to change spark plugs  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.  But it's entirely up to you.  Here's some more discussion of this subject:    <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/004407.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/004407.html</A> <P>Warning: <BR>Please, do <B>not</B> ask us what brand of spark plug to use.  Stick with less controversial subjects, like RU486, religion, politics, etc., and we'll get along just fine  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">    ||||reg||4
Z||000004||RossT||10-24-2000||11:57 PM||||Now Stu.. That was funny!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||wkayl||10-25-2000||12:18 AM||||It's very easy to change the plugs on the v6.  Just stand in the trunk, reach around from the rear (left) side of the engine.  You can reach all the way to the front easily.||||reg||1
Z||000006||bHooper||10-25-2000||10:33 AM||||Just curious, if you change the ignition module,pick up coil, cap and rotor, wouldn't you have to reset the timing?<P>That has been the only thing preventing me from doing so.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000007||william_thorniii||10-25-2000||10:42 AM||||Hoop,<P>I haven't ever touched my timing when replacing elecrical components (excluding distributor of course).  Am I missing something?  <P>The way that I see it, they only supply spark per the engines specifications and therefore no timing adjustments should be made.  Anyone else?  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000008||TheFiero64||10-25-2000||10:46 AM||||I had that stupid dist. o-ring go on me a wile ago. When they go they really leak.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||StuGood||10-25-2000||11:05 AM||||WT III, since you have to pull out the distributor to change the pickup coil, the timing has to be set.  Not changed from where it was, necessarily- but it does have to be set.<P>I think that's what bHooper meant (?)<P>You can change cap, rotor, and ignition module without disturbing the timing.  But if you change o-ring and/or pickup coil, you must pull the distributor out, and therefore you risk changing the timing.  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 10-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||bHooper||10-25-2000||11:35 AM||||i had the understanding that if you messed with the distributor at all, you had to set the timing.  Hence my paranoia of doing so.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fret Naught||10-25-2000||03:10 PM||||I think, I am not sure, that when you re install the distributer, you need to set the initial timing? then the ecm takes care of the rest. My question is if the distributer o ring is shot, will there be any evidence (oil) visible on the top of the engine around the distributer shaft? or is all the oil underneath?||||reg||14
Z||000012||TheFiero64||10-25-2000||05:24 PM||||It will be all over the engine around the base of the distributer.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||1
